/* Golden test for phx_mbcache.{h,c} — contract row 11 of
 * docs/BYTE_EXACT_FUNCTIONS.md.
 *
 * Golden-first: every expected array below was produced by an instrumented
 * build of the authoritative x264 C sources (reference/x264/common/macroblock.c
 * :673/:855/:1680, base.h:180), not by Phoenix itself.  The temporary harness
 * #includes x264's macroblock.c so the static macroblock_cache_load() is
 * reachable, zero-initialises x264_t, and calls the real x264 cache load/save
 * on a fixed 4x2 MB sequence with known types/MVs/nnz.  It links no x264
 * objects; mc/mem helpers are stubbed because they do not touch cache state.
 *
 *   cl -nologo -O2 /DBIT_DEPTH=8 "/Dx264_template(w)=x264_glue3(x264,8,w)" \
 *      /D__STDC_CONSTANT_MACROS /DHAVE_CONFIG_H \
 *      /I reference/x264 /I reference/x264/common /I reference/x264/encoder \
 *      /I src golden.c
 *   link -nologo /OUT:golden.exe golden.obj
 *
 * Each record asserts Phoenix's left/top/top-left/top-right cache arrays
 * (intra4x4_pred_mode, non_zero_count, ref, mv, mvd) byte-for-byte against
 * x264, the availability flags, and the per-MB state written by cache_save.
 * Progressive, adaptive MBAFF and forced-field MBAFF are all covered.
 * x264 stores mvd as uint8[8][2] and Phoenix as int16[8][2]; the golden
 * stream stores the low byte of each logical MVD, which is exact for the
 * 0..33 range x264 clips to (common/quant.c mvd clip).
 *
 * SHADER: B — the cache arrays are the explicit boundary between the
 * per-MB neighbour workgroup and the entropy/state writer.
 */
